   {
    "text": "I did not submit a candidate. The drift looks like a possible new plan code, but the profile does not show what the six values are, so I can't tell whether they are a deliberate change or a vendor error.\n\n**What changed in the file:** Every column is still present, in the same order, with the same types. Only `plan_code` is off. Six of the 24 rows carry a value outside the contract's `[HMO, PPO, EPO, HDHP]` list. The profile's `codes` field lists only the four allowed values, so the six unfamiliar values are not shown anywhere I can see. Nothing else in the file breaks a rule.\n\n**Why I stopped:** Adding a new code means knowing its exact spelling, and a guess could let junk values through. Widening the rule to a pattern would also be a loosening the evidence doesn't support. The dry run of the unchanged contract gives: file rejected, 0 accepted, 6 rejected (`VALUE.NOT_ALLOWED:plan_code` \u00d76), and `FILE.BAD_ROW_RATIO` because 6/24 is above the 5% limit. So the whole file is currently rejected, not just quarantined.\n\n**What is needed to finish:** Someone with access to the file should check the six `plan_code` values. If they are a real new plan (for example a valid code the vendor has added), I can add exactly those values to `allowed` and validate and submit. If they are typos or junk, this is a vendor mistake and the right step is a resend request, not a contract change.",
